WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.040
If an odour is an ensemble of neurons, what happens if you make one without any

2
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.040
odour?

3
00:00:09.383 --> 00:00:14.543
Choi and colleagues expressed channelrhodopsin in subsets of piriform neurons in

4
00:00:14.543 --> 00:00:15.543
awake mice.

5
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:18.647
Channelrhodopsin is a light-gated channel.

6
00:00:19.707 --> 00:00:22.367
Light drives those neurons, whatever the nose is doing.

7
00:00:23.417 --> 00:00:25.937
In one group, the light was paired with a reward.

8
00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:27.817
In another, with a shock.

9
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:30.050
Pause and predict.

10
00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:32.090
What will each group learn?

11
00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.350
Would that show the mice smelled something?

12
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:43.133
Paired with water, the light came to trigger licking.

13
00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:45.993
Paired with shock, it came to trigger flight.

14
00:00:46.983 --> 00:00:51.463
Small ensembles were enough, though they gave a weaker response than larger ones.

15
00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:56.477
Mice could also learn to tell different activated groups of neurons apart.

16
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.100
So activity in these neurons is sufficient as a learned cue.

17
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.960
Paired with an outcome, it was enough to learn an association.

18
00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.550
It does not show that the mice smelled anything.

19
00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:10.870
The authors withheld that claim.

20
00:01:11.883 --> 00:01:16.883
Nor does it test where the association is stored, in piriform or in the areas it

21
00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:17.883
feeds.

22
00:01:18.217 --> 00:01:19.517
Now the last puzzle.

23
00:01:20.457 --> 00:01:21.997
Does an odour's pattern stay put?

24
00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:27.527
Schoonover and colleagues recorded single piriform neurons over days to weeks.

25
00:01:28.567 --> 00:01:29.907
They used a decoder.

26
00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:34.987
It is a program trained to guess which odour was given, from the recorded activity.

27
00:01:36.117 --> 00:01:39.557
Its score is compared with chance, the score of random guessing.

28
00:01:40.617 --> 00:01:41.837
Pause and predict.

29
00:01:43.037 --> 00:01:44.417
Train the decoder on the first day.

30
00:01:45.257 --> 00:01:46.557
How well will it do a month later?

31
00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:53.687
It approached chance.

32
00:01:54.587 --> 00:01:56.067
The odour responses had drifted.

33
00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.570
Daily exposure to an odour slowed the drift.

34
00:02:00.583 --> 00:02:03.143
Drift does not mean the animal's smell changed.

35
00:02:03.983 --> 00:02:06.423
A decoder is a tool the experimenter holds.

36
00:02:07.483 --> 00:02:11.263
A downstream circuit may read a moving population consistently.

37
00:02:12.483 --> 00:02:17.223
If decoders retrained each day still work, the information is there, but the mapping

38
00:02:17.223 --> 00:02:21.183
changed. That reading needs checks that the same cells were tracked.

39
00:02:22.183 --> 00:02:27.123
So an odour does not always light the same set of cortical neurons, at least not in

40
00:02:27.123 --> 00:02:28.123
these recordings.

41
00:02:28.867 --> 00:02:32.727
What stays stable on top of a moving code is still an open question.

42
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:38.100
A scattered pattern can be taught meaning, and the pattern itself moves over weeks.
